foreign ministers from the association  of Southeast Asian Nations or asean meet  to discuss ways to resolve the crisis in  Myanmar its military rulers had agreed  to a peace plan with other asean member  states in April last year to end the  unrest the countries faced since they  took power in a coup last year  but there's been no progress and the  talks in Jakarta appeared to have failed  to change that  but it is too early to mention in detail  about the recommendations  because once again the task of this  meeting is to prepare for the next  month's Summit in Phnom Peng which among  other things will have a special session  to discuss the implementation of the  five-point consensus  the consensus calls for an immediate end  to violence and dialogue between  myanmar's Junta and anti-koo opponents  but the conflict is worsening this video  shows the aftermath of airstrikes by  myanmar's military last weekend they  struck a concert in kachin state a  stronghold of the kitchen Independence  Army one of myanmar's major ethnic armed  resistance groups at least 50 people  were killed  asean is under pressure to take a  stronger stance against myanmar's  military leaders  if I were to be in the position of the  foreign ministers I would  first convey to the untha that within  three months or before the end of this  year  if there were to be no positive reaction  and pigmentation on the part of the  Kathmandu  then the first thing  would be the suspension of Myanmar the  organization has also been urged to  engage with myanmar's so-called Shadow  government opposed to the coup  for those inside Myanmar it's hard to  remain optimistic  we've been moving towards a military  solution  internally domestically  um  the options for a political solution  are running out or they never existed at  all  the Junta plans to hold an election next  year but it's being criticized by many  as a way to Grant legitimacy to military  rule  Florence Louis Al Jazeera
 

